    <!-- ===================================================== pairing
         Section 5.15. Three things reach the machine and only two of them are
         entered here: the address and the passphrase are credentials the phone
         stores, and the third is this phone\'s own key, which has to be added at
         the other end because no amount of typing on a phone can put it there.

         The two captions are deliberately the same sentence written in opposite
         directions. The tailcat address looks like an address and is a key, so its
         caption says so before the user pastes it into a chat window. The phone key
         looks like a secret and is not one, so its caption says that too, because a
         user who treats it as a secret will not put it on the allow list and
         nothing will ever connect.

         The passphrase has no string of its own here on purpose: it is entered
         through `m8_auth_entry_label` and `m8_auth_entry_caption`, section 5.5\'s
         existing pair, and it is never displayed, never logged and never echoed
         back. `m8_pair_address_stored` is what stands in for showing it: the field
         reads empty on a return visit because the value is stored, not because it
         was lost, and without that line an empty field is indistinguishable from a
         failure.

         Forgetting is the one thing in M8 that asks first. `m8_pair_forget_action`
         opens a sheet carrying `m8_pair_forget_title`, `m8_pair_forget_body` and
         the two controls in that reading order, with the destructive one the
         quieter of the two, which is M3's `m3_locked_discard_action` rule and not
         a slip. `m8_pair_forget_body` names the real cost, which is that the
         address embeds a pre-shared key and has to be fetched from the machine
         again; `AgentSecrets.clear()` leaves the node key alone, which is why the
         last sentence says so. -->
    <string name="m8_pair_title">Connect Maia to your machine</string>
