    <!-- ============================== before the first list, and a stale one
         Section 5.16, and the point of these eight is that three situations which
         all render as "no list" are three different facts and are kept apart:
         Maia has never had a list, Maia has a list it cannot refresh, and the
         machine answered and said it has none. `registry-serve.py` answers 503
         naming the cause rather than serving an empty list precisely so the third
         is distinguishable, and the phone throwing that away would make the one
         case where Maia knows more look like the case where it knows least.

         `m8_list_stale_note` says what actually goes stale, and no more. Numbers
         are allocated once and never reused, so a held number is still exactly the
         project it was; only projects added since the fetch are missing. Saying the
         numbering may be stale would be false, and would make a user hesitate to
         say "seven" on the one screen whose entire premise is that the number is
         the primary key.

         `m8_list_stale_no_registry_note` is the fourth state: a list from last
         time and a refresh that came back 503. It replaces `m8_list_stale_note` on
         that line rather than sitting beside it, because the stale note's second
         sentence implies a refresh would fix this and on a 503 that is false until
         somebody runs the script on the devbox. `m8_list_registry_path` is reused
         beneath it and `m8_list_sync_action` stays. -->
    <string name="m8_list_first_sync_title">Getting your project list.</string>
    <string name="m8_list_first_sync_body">The first one takes a moment. After this Maia keeps a copy, so the list is there even when your machine is not.</string>
    <string name="m8_list_none_yet_title">No project list yet.</string>
    <string name="m8_list_none_yet_body">Maia has never had a list from your machine, so there is nothing to show and nothing older to fall back on.</string>
